Summarize the following news article in one detailed paragraph covering all key facts, names and figures.

Article:
(CNN)Five Americans who were monitored for three weeks at an Omaha, Nebraska, hospital after being exposed to Ebola in West Africa have been released, a Nebraska Medicine spokesman said in an email Wednesday. One of the five had a heart-related issue on Saturday and has been discharged but hasn't left the area, Taylor Wilson wrote. The others have already gone home. They were exposed to Ebola in Sierra Leone in March, but none developed the deadly virus. They are clinicians for Partners in Health, a Boston-based aid group. They all had contact with a colleague who was diagnosed with the disease and is being treated at the National Institutes of Health in Bethesda, Maryland. As of Monday, that health care worker is in fair condition. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention in Atlanta has said the last of 17 patients who were being monitored are expected to be released by Thursday. More than 10,000 people have died in a West African epidemic of Ebola that dates to December 2013, according to the World Health Organization. Almost all the deaths have been in Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone. Ebola is spread by direct contact with the bodily fluids of an infected person.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
Here is a detailed paragraph summarizing the news article:

Five American clinicians who were exposed to Ebola in Sierra Leone in March while working for the Boston-based aid group Partners in Health have been released from an Omaha, Nebraska, hospital after being monitored for three weeks. The group, which had contact with a colleague who was diagnosed with the disease, was being treated at the hospital as a precautionary measure, but fortunately, none of them developed the deadly virus. One of the five individuals had a heart-related issue on Saturday, but has since been discharged and is still in the area. The others have already returned home. The colleague who was diagnosed with Ebola is being treated at the National Institutes of Health in Bethesda, Maryland, and is currently in fair condition. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) has stated that the last of 17 patients being monitored are expected to be released by Thursday. The outbreak, which began in West Africa in December 2013, has resulted in over 10,000 deaths, with the majority of cases occurring in Guinea, Liberia, and Sierra Leone.<|eot_id|>



